             How to `Gate' to FidoNet Nodes via. Internet E-Mail
             ===================================================
                       (known as e-mail tunnelling)

Fidonet via e-mail is an area of Fidonet which is growing daily.  While not
having anywhere near the speed of a dial up connection, Fidonet via e-mail
provides a way to cheaply distribute networks over long distances, such as
international links.

EMailGate is a program which allows the sending of normal Fidonet mail over
e-mail which, once at the destination, can be decoded back into real Fidonet
bundles and packets.  This is known as tunnelling Fidonet style mail over
e-mail.

Zeus does not current provide facilities for decoding mail, although this is
easily done by running a uudecoder and MIME decoder over a specifically named
mailbox.  We will endevour to expand Zeus BBSs features to include a purpose
built decoder soon (one which will allow easy integration with shared
mailboxes, if you only have a single e-mail address, for example).


Usage
=====

EMailGate can either place tunnelled mail in the UUSpool: assign, ready for
sending with SMTPpost (please see the script provided below), or it can use
PutMail by Martin Lanza to directly send mail to your Internet provider's
servers.  PutMail is available on AmiNet.

If the mail is placed in UUSpool:, then it is in a raw e-mail format.  This
is suitable for sending via any other means, either by importing directly
into a package such as YAM or even processed by some ARexx for sending by any
method at all.

If you wish to use PutMail, you should have a directory PutMail in AmiTCP:
in which PutMail is place (i.e., "AmiTCP:PutMail/PutMail" is the executable).
If you use Miami or another TCP/IP stack, you will have to assign AmiTCP: to
your Miami directory and place the PutMail directory in there.  This is
problem which will be readdressed.


Configuration
=============

1) Edit Zeus:Config/EmailTunnel.cfg :

   The first line is an identification of the configuration being used, and
   is ideally used to put a short identifier to highlight that the messages
   being generated contain UUEncoded Fidonet-type mail.

   To gate to three nodes, an example config might be:

   ----------------cut-----------------
   FIDONET
   85:1/467.0   someone@address.com
   85:1/543.0   another@hello.com
   2:1/344.0   another@blah.com
   ----------------cut-----------------


2) Load up Prefs/Archivers and add a new archiver "UUENCODE"

   Enter data for your UUEncoder (UUxt is recommended) so that `Compress'
   uuencodes data, and `Decompress' uudecodes data.  Since this is useless
   to general BBS use, deselect `Show User'.

   For example:

   Name:      UUENCODE
   Compress:  c:uuxt a ~a ~f
   Extract:   c:uuxt x ~a
   Show User: NO 

   Leave the other fields blank.

   Run "UpdateConfigs ARC" to update this.


3) Using PUTMAIL:

   To use PutMail, include the PUTMAIL argument when running the program.

   My email address for Fidonet is "fido@freeserve.co.uk" and my SMTPHOST is
   "smtp.freeserve.co.uk" so I would use the following command line.

   Run Zeus:Bin/INetGate CONFIGFILE Zeus:Config/EmailTunnel.cfg MAILADDRESS fido@tkgbbs.freeserve.co.uk SMTPHOST smtp.freeserve.co.uk ARCHIVER UUENCODE PUTMAIL COPYOLD

   Demon configurations might use (for example):
 
   Run Zeus:Bin/INetGate CONFIGFILE Zeus:Config/EmailTunnel.cfg MAILADDRESS zeusnet@zeus.demon.co.uk SMTPHOST post.demon.co.uk ARCHIVER UUENCODE COPYOLD


   Using UUSpool:

   Run Zeus:Bin/INetGate CONFIGFILE Zeus:Config/EmailTunnel.cfg MAILADDRESS fido@tkgbbs.freeserve.co.uk SMTPHOST smtp.freeserve.co.uk ARCHIVER UUENCODE COPYOLD

   This will leave files in UUSpool: which can be sent using SMTPpost (from
   the INetUtils package on AmiNet) using a script such as:

------------ cut -------------------
; Send queue mail via SMTP

pcd uuspool:
List >T:SendMailQueue.temp D.#? lformat "SMTPPost -S %n*Nif not error*N delete %n*N delete X.%e*Nendif*N"
Execute T:SendMailQueue.temp
Delete T:SendMailQueue.temp
pcd
------------ cut -------------------

   Or processed however you wish.


4) COPYOLD

   This argument, on the command line, will copy mail which has been sent
   into a directory called "Old" in your outbound directory.  Should
   something go wrong and the mail is not delivered, you can resend it
   manulally from here.


